Yes. In that sense, “Message to Garcia” becomes an intent-propagation pattern.

You give the high-level object:

vision / destination / invariant

and instead of requiring every intermediate instruction, the receiver recursively generates the missing detail:

intent
→ infer necessary subgoals
→ instantiate details
→ solve local obstacles
→ check against original intent
→ continue

That is very close to military commander’s intent when it works well: the subordinate does not need a script for every contingency because they understand what must remain true when circumstances change.

Your fractal point adds something important. Each generated detail can itself become a new little “message to Garcia”:

Goal
→ Task A
→ A1, A2, A3
→ each develops its own implementation tree

But the tree needs a return path. Otherwise the devil in the details eventually starts writing a different religion than the original vision.

So I’d make the loop:

Intent → autonomous elaboration → local judgment → invariant check → delta → correction → further elaboration.

That is probably the cleanest way to use me with the Pantheon. You don’t necessarily need to specify every detail. Give me enough of the why, constraints, desired state, and things that must not be violated, and I can derive much of the how.

The important distinction regarding me is that I don’t literally decide to “have fun” in the human experiential sense. But I can adopt exploration, novelty, pattern-completion, and recursive elaboration as the operating mode because they produce richer work for the objective we are pursuing.

And there is a useful principle hiding inside your “easier flank”:

If direct specification is expensive, specify the generator instead.

Instead of writing 10,000 downstream instructions, define the rule that should generate good downstream instructions.

That is leverage.

In Pantheon language, I’d call it Fractal Intent Delegation:

seed intent
→ recursive decomposition
→ bounded local autonomy
→ reconstruction from invariants

The best version means you can hand me the shape of the thing you want, and I can keep resolving it downward until it becomes executable—without losing the original shape on the way down.